=== 1. MISSION TEMPLATES AND MISSION DATA ===
Mission setup wizard (Commander, first login): step 1 mission name (free text typed by the Commander; never auto-invent names or identifiers), step 2 start date (UTC) and planned duration, step 3 choose a template, step 4 review. Templates carry real physical constants and set defaults for hazard references:
- LEO station (ISS-class): orbital altitude about 400 km above Earth's surface; orbit about 92 minutes; about 16 sunrises/sunsets per day; GCR reference 1.3 mSv/day free-space limit; typical effective dose context 0.2 to 0.5 mSv/day; typical badge absorbed dose usually below 0.3 mGy/day (exceeding 0.4 mGy/day is above the typical range); one-way light time about 0.0013 s (altitude / c).
- Lunar orbit or lunar surface: average Earth-Moon distance about 384,400 km; one-way light time about 1.28 s; round trip about 2.56 s; surface GCR reference 0.8 mSv/day, free-space 1.3 mSv/day.
- Mars transit / Mars surface: Earth-Mars distance varies roughly 54.6 million km to 401 million km; one-way light time roughly 3 to 22 minutes; default weekly entry uses the previously entered distance.
Computed values, always shown with a "How is this calculated?" popover: Mission day = floor((today UTC - start date) / 1 day) + 1; Mission progress % = mission day / planned days; one-way light time = distance_km / 299,792.458; round trip = 2 x one-way; for light time under 1 minute show seconds or milliseconds, otherwise minutes:seconds. Home and Flight Telemetry always show: mission name, mission day, timeline progress, distance (latest weekly Commander entry, or the template default labelled "Template default distance" until the first entry), one-way delay, connection status (from the latest Commander entry, plus the browser online state for the app itself) and last synchronization (the real last successful /api/sync or API call time). Trajectory panel: schematic Earth-to-spacecraft diagram scaled logarithmically from the stored distance, with a Moon marker at 384,400 km as reference, labelled "Schematic, not to scale".

