=== 3. RADIATION SCENARIO AND RULES (Commander, weekly) ===
Form fields (units and validation on all): detector (list above), detector location/module (free text), measurement period start and end, absorbed dose rate (mGy/day) and equivalent/effective dose rate (mSv/day) as SEPARATE fields, accumulated mission absorbed dose (mGy), accumulated mission effective dose (mSv), prior career effective dose (mSv, entered once, default 0 shown as "None entered"), solar event status (None observed / SPE in progress / SPE ended), solar-event notes.
Computed: career total = prior career + mission accumulated effective dose; percent of the 600 mSv limit; remaining to 600 mSv; trailing 30-day effective dose from weekly entries (sum of period doses) vs the 250 mSv 30-day reference; daily average effective dose vs the template GCR reference.
Seeded rules:
- R-RAD-01 (INFORMATION, NASA REFERENCE context): absorbed dose rate > 0.4 mGy/day (above the typical LEO badge range). Next step: confirm detector reading and location, compare with a second instrument if available, record the source.
- R-RAD-02 (MONITOR, PROTOTYPE DEFAULT margin): career total >= 50% of 600 mSv or 30-day total >= 50% of 250 mSv.
- R-RAD-03 (ALERT, PROTOTYPE DEFAULT margin): career total >= 80% of 600 mSv or 30-day total >= 80% of 250 mSv. Next step: notify the Doctor and mission support; follow the mission's ALARA procedures.
- R-RAD-04 (ALERT, NASA REFERENCE): career total >= 600 mSv or 30-day total >= 250 mSv.
- R-RAD-05 (HIGH PRIORITY): solar event status = SPE in progress. Next step: follow the mission's approved solar particle event contingency procedures (shielding/shelter plan) and contact mission support. The Commander is alerted immediately; the Doctor receives an INFORMATION notice only.
Radiation page also has an educational panel with sources: NASA career limit 600 mSv (universal for age and sex), SPE reference < 250 mSv, ISS effective dose typically about 0.2 to 0.5 mSv/day, ISS-RAD and CAD roles. Gy/mGy and Sv/mSv are never converted into each other.

=== 4. ENVIRONMENT, GRAVITY, ISOLATION, DISTANCE RULES (Commander, weekly) ===
Environment protocol default: record ppCO2 samples every 15 minutes for at least 75 minutes (5 samples, spanning 60 minutes or more, no gap larger than 20 minutes) so a verified rolling one-hour average can be computed.
- R-ENV-01 (NASA REFERENCE, V2 6004): verified one-hour average ppCO2 > 3 mmHg -> ALERT. Next step: check ventilation and CO2 removal status on the approved vehicle displays, re-sample, notify mission support; crew reporting symptoms should contact the Doctor.
- R-ENV-02 (INFORMATION): a single ppCO2 sample > 3 mmHg while coverage is incomplete -> "Single sample above 3 mmHg; the one-hour average cannot be verified yet. Continue sampling."
- R-ENV-03 (INFORMATION, NASA TB-004 context): 7-day average ppCO2 > 2.5 mmHg (NASA brief links keeping the 7-day average below 2.5 mmHg to a headache risk below 1%).
- R-ENV-04 (MONITOR): ppO2 outside 145 to 155 mmHg (NASA normoxia target range, V2 6003). Next step: verify the sensor reading, check atmosphere control status, notify mission support.
- R-ENV-05 (ALERT): total pressure outside 34.5 to 103 kPa (V2 6006) or diluent gas < 30% (V2 6002).
- R-ENV-06: temperature or humidity outside the crew performance zone (20 to 25 C, 30 to 60% RH, V2 6013) but inside the crew health limits -> INFORMATION; outside the crew health limits (18 C at 75% RH to 27 C at 25% RH, V2 6012) -> ALERT. Implement the health limits as the line between the two stated corner points.
- R-ENV-07 (MONITOR): ventilation status Degraded or Off.
- R-GRV-01 (INFORMATION, never punitive): fewer than 6 exercise days in the week versus the NASA ISS reference of 2.5-hour blocks six days a week (1.5 h resistive + 1 h aerobic); one rest day per week is part of the reference. Show per-crew weekly exercise minutes to the Commander as non-ranked totals only if the crew member logged them; no comparison between people.
- R-ISO-01 (INFORMATION, PROTOTYPE DEFAULT): more than 7 days since last ground contact. Isolation view shows ground-contact sessions count, days since last contact and aggregate-only crew counts; never individual mood or stress to the Commander.
- R-DST-01 (MONITOR): communication status Degraded. R-DST-02 (ALERT): Loss of signal. Next step: follow the mission's communication-loss procedures; use locally stored protocols; the app keeps working offline and queues entries.

=== 5. CREW HEALTH RULES (daily, notify the DOCTOR) ===
Seeded as ACTIVE. Each stores rule id, title, category, severity, condition, label, source link, approved next step. Heart rate rules apply only to context = Resting.
- R-HR-01 (MONITOR, CLINICAL REFERENCE: normal adult resting heart rate 60 to 100 bpm): resting HR < 60 or > 100. Next step: re-measure after 5 minutes of rest using the same device, then record again.
- R-HR-02 (REVIEW, PROTOTYPE DEFAULT): two resting readings outside 60 to 100 bpm on consecutive days. Doctor notified.
- R-BP-01 (MONITOR, ACC/AHA 2017 categories): systolic >= 130 or diastolic >= 80. R-BP-02 (REVIEW): systolic >= 140 or diastolic >= 90. R-BP-03 (HIGH PRIORITY): systolic > 180 or diastolic > 120. R-BP-04 (MONITOR, general reference): systolic < 90 or diastolic < 60. Next steps: follow the BP measurement protocol (seated, rested 5 minutes, arm at heart level, two readings one minute apart), repeat the reading, contact the Doctor for REVIEW or HIGH PRIORITY.
- R-SLP-01 (MONITOR, CLINICAL REFERENCE: AASM/SRS consensus, adults should regularly sleep at least 7 hours; NASA schedules 8.5 hours): sleep < 7.0 h. R-SLP-02 (REVIEW, PROTOTYPE DEFAULT): three consecutive nights < 7.0 h. R-SLP-03 (INFORMATION): a downward trend of 3 or more consecutive decreasing steps. The sleep chart always draws two reference lines: 7 h (clinical reference) and 8.5 h (NASA scheduled sleep period). Check with the five-night example 7.5, 7.2, 6.8, 6.1, 5.9 h: nights 3, 4 and 5 are below 7.0 h -> MONITOR on night 3, REVIEW to the Doctor on night 5; nights 1 and 2 raise nothing; the INFORMATION trend appears after night 4 (three decreasing steps).
- R-MOOD-01 (REVIEW, PROTOTYPE DEFAULT): mood level 1 (Very low) on two consecutive days -> Doctor notified privately. R-STR-01 (MONITOR, PROTOTYPE DEFAULT): stress >= 70/100 on a day. R-STR-02 (REVIEW): stress >= 70 on three consecutive days. Instrument label for both: "Self-report scale, prototype instrument v1".
- R-ACT-01 (INFORMATION, never punitive): weekly exercise below the NASA reference schedule; shown only to the crew member and Doctor as a neutral note.
- R-DATA-01 (INFORMATION to Doctor): two consecutive days without daily entries. R-DATA-02 (UNKNOWN category to the Commander): weekly hazard entry overdue by 1 day or more.
- R-REP-01 (REVIEW or HIGH PRIORITY): crew "Report a problem" form; Urgent -> HIGH PRIORITY to all Doctors immediately via SSE.
