=== 14. LANGUAGE: ENGLISH AND ARABIC WITH FULL RTL ===
Architecture
- Lightweight i18n layer (react-i18next acceptable): /locales/en.json and /locales/ar.json with namespaced keys, ICU-style plurals and interpolation. NO hard-coded user-facing strings anywhere (toasts, errors, empty states, aria-labels, chart alt text, document titles, placeholders, print headers). This includes ALL knowledge-base content from sections 1 to 9: rule titles, explanations and next steps, protocol titles and steps, education cards and quizzes, achievement names, template names, instrument descriptions. `npm run i18n:check` fails on a key missing in one file or a hard-coded JSX string.
- Switching language updates instantly without reload, sets <html lang="en|ar"> and <html dir="ltr|rtl">, updates document.title and persists per section 11.
- Backend stays language-neutral: errors return { "error": "English fallback", "code": "STABLE_CODE", "field"?: "name", "params"?: {} } and the client translates by code. Alerts, notifications, task titles and audit-log lines are stored as template key + params and rendered in the VIEWER's language. Doctor-authored free text (rule guidance edits, resolution notes) and user free text (problem reports) display exactly as typed with a small "Original text" label and are never machine-translated.
- Do not translate: NASA standard identifiers and requirement tags (NASA-STD-3001, [V2 6004]), document titles and URLs, instrument names and acronyms (CAD, ISS-RAD, REM2, HERA, TEPC), unit symbols (bpm, mmHg, kPa, mSv, mGy, uSv/h, km, C/F). Status codes show a translated label and expose the canonical English code in a tooltip and in the accessible description so safety-critical meaning is never lost.
- Mandatory safety sentences must exist in both languages with identical meaning: the prototype disclaimer; "No approved rule is configured for this indicator. ASTRA does not diagnose or interpret readings."; "No approved guidance is configured for this condition. Contact the designated support role."; "Approved device instructions are not configured. Contact the designated support role."; "ASTRA is read-only. It cannot control spacecraft environmental systems."; "No alert is active."; "Prototype default protocol. Adapt to the devices and procedures approved for your mission."; "Schematic, not to scale". Use clear Modern Standard Arabic (formal, medical-neutral, no dialect). Machine-drafted Arabic strings are listed with "reviewed": false in a separate /locales/review-status.json so a human can review them.
RTL layout
- CSS logical properties everywhere (margin-inline-start, padding-inline-end, inset-inline-start, text-align: start, border-start-start-radius); no left/right in layout CSS.
- In RTL: sidebar on the right, bottom nav order mirrors, breadcrumbs and Back arrows flip (mirrored icon, never a reversed text arrow), toasts and modals stay centered, dropdowns open toward the correct side, the calendar grid mirrors (Monday at the right edge), progress bars fill from the right, sliders and steppers mirror.
- LTR islands that must not mirror: numeric clinical values (118/76, 72 bpm), times and dates in UTC format, unit symbols, email addresses, invite codes, NASA identifiers, and SVG charts (time axis left-to-right, y-axis labels on the left; chart titles, legends and captions are translated and follow the page direction). Use <bdi> or dir="ltr" with unicode-bidi: isolate so mixed Arabic/Latin text never scrambles.
- Fonts: Arabic headings in "Cairo" (Anton has no Arabic glyphs), Arabic body in "IBM Plex Sans Arabic" or "Noto Sans Arabic", with fallback stacks; Latin keeps Anton/Inter. Arabic line-height 1.7, no letter-spacing on Arabic. font-display: swap, only the needed weights.
- Formatting with Intl.DateTimeFormat / NumberFormat / PluralRules / RelativeTimeFormat using the active locale (en-US or ar-EG-u-nu-latn by default; Arabic-Indic digits only when the Numerals preference says so and only for non-clinical text). All dates and times stay in UTC and are labelled "UTC" / «بالتوقيت العالمي».
- Reports and exports respect the language chosen at export time, including column header labels; machine field names in JSON stay in English.
Switcher: segmented control in Settings > Appearance plus a compact switcher (EN | ع) on Login, Register and in the top bar. Language names always appear in their own language ("English", "العربية").

=== 15. DATA EXPORT ===
Who can export what (enforced on the server, audit-logged):
- CREW: only their own data.
- DOCTOR: their own data plus the health data of a selected crew member or the whole crew (every export of another person's data writes an audit_log entry "Dr X exported Y's data"). Includes alerts, problem reports, rules and Doctor rule changes, baselines. Hazard data is NEVER included for the Doctor.
- COMMANDER: their own data plus a mission export: roster and roles, compliance (done / not done per task, never psychological values), weekly hazard entries, hazard analysis results, alerts summary, calendar/tasks, audit log. Individual mood, stress and isolation values of other people are NEVER included.
UI (Settings > Data & Privacy > "Export my data"):
1. Scope (role-aware): My data / Selected crew member / Entire crew (Doctor) / Mission (Commander).
2. Date range: Last 7 days, Last 30 days, Entire mission, Custom (UTC dates).
3. Content checklist (only what the role may export): Daily readings, Activity sessions, Alerts and notifications, Problem reports, Tasks and deferrals, Baselines, Weekly hazard entries (Commander only), Audit log (Commander/Doctor, own scope), Preferences, Reference rules, protocols and sources.
4. Format: JSON (single file), CSV (ZIP with one CSV per table plus README.txt), Printable report (opens the Reports print view).
5. Label language: English or Arabic (default = current UI language). CSV files use UTF-8 with BOM so Arabic opens correctly in Excel.
6. "Estimated size" and a "Contains sensitive personal health data" warning with a required checkbox "I understand this file is not encrypted" before the Export button is enabled.
Behavior:
- POST /api/exports {scope, subject_user_id?, from, to, include[], format, label_language} creates an export; small exports stream immediately; exports above 5,000 rows run as a background job with status PENDING / READY / FAILED and a progress indicator; GET /api/exports lists the user's last 20 exports (created time, scope, format, size, status); GET /api/exports/:id/download returns the file with Content-Disposition and the correct MIME type; exports expire and are deleted after 24 hours; the download URL is authenticated, never public.
- File names: astra-{scope}-{YYYYMMDD-HHmm}Z.{json|zip}.
- Every export begins with a metadata block: app name, app version, schema version, exported_at (ISO 8601 UTC), exported_by (name, role), scope, date range, included sections, label language, data_provenance counts (Self-reported, Manual entry, Offline-queued), the prototype disclaimer, and the line "Values are exactly as entered. Nothing has been estimated or extrapolated."
- Honesty: missing values export as null with a status field (UNKNOWN / STALE / INVALID / NOT_CONFIGURED / INSUFFICIENT DATA), never as 0 or empty strings; Gy/mGy and Sv/mSv columns stay separate and are never converted; units are exported in base units with an explicit unit column, independent of display-unit preference; timestamps as ISO 8601 UTC plus epoch milliseconds; each record keeps its source, detector/device ID, data quality and provenance.
- CSV injection guard: prefix cell values beginning with =, +, -, @ or tab with an apostrophe.
- Rate limit: 5 exports per hour per user. Offline: the Export button is disabled with an explanation, except "Export unsynced queue", which downloads the local IndexedDB queue as JSON.
- "Download my data package" in Account settings exports everything the user may export, in JSON.

